{"hash":"cc6f09db11d46ac8","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":85.658,"said":"Pause at the head of these steps. Ganga came down from Brahmagiri, the Purana says, at a place called Gangadwar, flowing from the branches of a fig tree. But when Gautama’s old accusers came to bathe, she vanished. Gautama pleaded with her to forgive them, and in the end they walked around the mountain to atone. Then Gautama made a tank below Gangadwar, where the river would gather. That tank is Kushavarta, a short walk from here in the town; many say its name comes from the kusha grass with which the sage held the river back. Every Monday a palanquin carries the Lord’s mask from this temple to Kushavarta for worship. And every twelve years, when Jupiter enters Leo, the gods’ promise is kept as the Simhastha Kumbh, and the sadhus’ holiest bath is taken at Kushavarta; the next Simhastha opens at the end of October 2026. Families also come here all year for rites for their ancestors, such as the Narayan Nagbali, which tradition holds belongs to this place. Remember Varuna’s words: rites for the ancestors offered here would never perish. Now step down. The Lord is just below.\n\nDid you know? Kalsarp shanti and Tripindi shraddha are also performed at Trimbakeshwar, by hereditary tirtha purohits of the town.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1089},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1105,"end":1221}],"words":[[0.098,0.264,0,5],[0.374,0.096,6,8],[0.483,0.108,9,12],[0.603,0.228,13,17],[0.843,0.12,18,20],[0.976,0.228,21,26],[1.216,0.493,27,32],[2.62,0.445,34,39],[3.077,0.312,40,44],[3.401,0.264,45,49],[3.678,0.204,50,54],[3.894,0.649,55,65],[4.724,0.096,67,70],[4.832,0.481,71,77],[5.324,0.433,78,82],[5.998,0.168,84,86],[6.178,0.048,87,88],[6.238,0.361,89,94],[6.611,0.3,95,101],[6.923,0.757,102,111],[7.909,0.433,113,120],[8.353,0.192,121,125],[8.558,0.084,126,129],[8.654,0.577,130,138],[9.243,0.144,139,141],[9.399,0.06,142,143],[9.471,0.24,144,147],[9.724,0.385,148,152],[10.